Q: Splitting a number in half but fixing when second half starts with 0 A: <p>As an easily understood approach, you can calculate the midpoint of the string of single byte characters, then split the string at the position which is zero or more <code>0</code>s after the midpoint. <a href="https://3v4l.org/oRJDn#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>$string = '5639445604728832';

$mid = ceil(strlen($string) / 2);

var_export(
    preg_split(&quot;#^.{{$mid}}0*\K#&quot;, $string)
);
</code></pre>
<p>For a less understandable script that avoids pre-calculating the midpoint of the string, call <code>preg_split()</code> with a recursive pattern (which I adapted from Casimir's solution). This uses a conditional subexpression with a backreference to a capture group inside a lookahead as the regex tranverses the input string from the left side. Fundamentally,  it matches from the start the equivalent number of characters from the end (allowing the left side to have the spare character), then matches zero or more zeros, then forgets all of the matched characters with <code>\K</code>. <a href="https://3v4l.org/ouNoi#v" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>var_export(
    preg_split('#^.?(?:.(?=.*(.(?(1)\1)$)))+0*\K#', $string)
);
</code></pre>
<p>Output (from either script):</p>
<pre><code>array (
  0 =&gt; '563944560',
  1 =&gt; '4728832',
)
</code></pre>
